---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { CORE_COMMIT_SHORT, CORE_RELEASE_TAG, milestone, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Language Reference"
  description="Where RoboLang is specified: the language 1.1 to 2.2 specifications, grammars, Task and Mission IR schemas, simulation semantics, the goal bridge and the experimental Core 0.1 and 0.2 contracts, each linked at a fixed version in the core repository."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="03" label="Language Reference" title="Language reference">
    The specifications live in the RoboLang core repository. This page points to them at a fixed version and gives a short orientation.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>The documents linked here are the authority.</strong> This website does not restate the
        grammar or the rules. If anything on this site disagrees with a specification, the
        specification is right. All links point to core commit <code>{CORE_COMMIT_SHORT}</code>, release
        tag <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code>, the version this site was checked against.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Specifications</h2>
  <ul class="cards">
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.languageReference}>Language reference</a></h3>
      <p>
        Everything a task author needs on one page, tested against the tools. The installed package
        prints the same text with <code>robolang reference</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language}>Language specification 1.1</a></h3>
      <p>
        Source text, tokens, grammar, static rules, the full list of diagnostics, the abstract syntax
        tree and compatibility with earlier RoboLang sources.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language12}>Language specification 1.2</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.2 adds to 1.1: conditions combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and
        <code>not</code>, evaluated in three-valued logic.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language13}>Language specification 1.3</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.3 adds: a required <code>goal</code> block that decides success, the outcome conditions
        <code>object_at</code>, <code>robot_at</code> and <code>holding</code>, and typed
        <code>sensor</code> parameters.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language14}>Language specification 1.4</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.4 adds: bounded <code>if</code> / <code>else if</code> / <code>else</code> in plans,
        with a goal per branch, and <code>fail "reason";</code> for branches that report and stop.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language15}>Language specification 1.5</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.5 adds: a failure handler on a step, <code>&lt;step&gt; else &#123; … &#125;</code>,
        which runs only when that step fails.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language16}>Language specification 1.6</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.6 adds: <code>measure</code>, typed values with units bound to a name, and comparisons
        in conditions. A reading near a threshold is unknown and fails closed.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language17}>Language specification 1.7</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.7 adds: region limits, <code>@safety no_go_region</code> and <code>in_region</code>.
        Requests checked against the world, never enforced by RoboLang.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language18}>Language specification 1.8</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.8 adds: declared actions with <code>do</code>, from the robot's profile, and the
        condition <code>in_state</code> to verify their result.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language19}>Language specification 1.9</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 1.9 adds: clock readings, the local time of day and the time elapsed, read with
        <code>measure</code> and compared in conditions.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language20}>Language specification 2.0</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 2.0 adds: the mission layer. A <code>mission</code> repeats bounded, verified task runs
        with a hard bound, an optional <code>until</code>, and <code>stop</code>,
        <code>continue</code> or <code>retry N</code> on a failed run.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language21}>Language specification 2.1</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 2.1 adds: incoming messages, <code>message_received "CH"</code> with an optional exact
        text, in a <code>pre</code>, an <code>if</code>, a <code>wait until</code> or a mission's
        <code>until</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.language22}>Language specification 2.2</a></h3>
      <p>
        What 2.2 adds: four more kinds for <code>measure</code>, <code>fill</code> and
        <code>confidence</code> in <code>%</code>, <code>pressure</code>, and an exact
        <code>count</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Grammars (EBNF)</h3>
      <p>
        The syntactic and lexical grammar in ISO-style EBNF, including the reserved words:
        <a href={specs.grammar}>1.1</a>, <a href={specs.grammar12}>1.2</a>,
        <a href={specs.grammar13}>1.3</a>, <a href={specs.grammar14}>1.4</a>,
        <a href={specs.grammar15}>1.5</a>, <a href={specs.grammar16}>1.6</a>,
        <a href={specs.grammar17}>1.7</a>, <a href={specs.grammar18}>1.8</a>,
        <a href={specs.grammar19}>1.9</a>, <a href={specs.grammar20}>2.0</a>,
        <a href={specs.grammar21}>2.1</a> and <a href={specs.grammar22}>2.2</a>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>IR schemas</h3>
      <p>
        The versioned JSON produced by <code>robolang inspect --json</code>. Task IR
        <a href={specs.irSchema}>1.0</a>, <a href={specs.irSchema11}>1.1</a>,
        <a href={specs.irSchema12}>1.2</a>, <a href={specs.irSchema13}>1.3</a>,
        <a href={specs.irSchema14}>1.4</a>, <a href={specs.irSchema15}>1.5</a>,
        <a href={specs.irSchema16}>1.6</a>, <a href={specs.irSchema17}>1.7</a>,
        <a href={specs.irSchema18}>1.8</a>, <a href={specs.irSchema19}>1.9</a> and
        <a href={specs.irSchema110}>1.10</a>, and
        <a href={specs.missionIr}>Mission IR</a> {versions.missionIr} for files with missions. The
        <a href={specs.irReader}>IR reader</a> defines how <code>--ir</code> reads it back and
        re-validates it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.simulation}>Simulation semantics</a></h3>
      <p>
        What <code>robolang simulate</code> does: executor states, goal checking, decisions in
        branching plans, missions, world file {versions.worldFile}, run record {versions.runRecord}
        and mission record {versions.missionRecord}.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <p>
        The recorded design decisions behind the specifications, for example
        <a href={specs.decisions}>language 1.1</a>, <a href={specs.decisionsLang12}>1.2</a>,
        <a href={specs.decisionsLang13}>1.3</a>, <a href={specs.decisionsLang14}>1.4</a>,
        <a href={specs.decisionsLang15}>1.5</a>, <a href={specs.decisionsLang16}>1.6</a>,
        <a href={specs.decisionsLang17}>1.7</a>, <a href={specs.decisionsLang18}>1.8</a>,
        <a href={specs.decisionsLang19}>1.9</a>, <a href={specs.decisionsLang20}>2.0</a>,
        <a href={specs.decisionsLang21}>2.1</a> and <a href={specs.decisionsLang22}>2.2</a>. All of
        them are in the <a href={specs.decisionsDir}>decision records</a>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Experimental: Core {versions.core}</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Core defines what AI agents and robots exchange, separately from the <code>.rob</code> language.
      Core {versions.core} are drafts, implemented as per-message checks and exercised in simulation
      only, by <code>export-goal</code>, <code>delegate</code> and the reference simulation. Core
      {versions.coreDefault} is the default; <code>--core 0.2</code> opts in. An independent Core 0.2
      implementation passed the Gate 2 interoperability test on {milestone.date}. Core is not a
      network protocol, and a valid Core document is never permission to operate a robot.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ul class="cards">
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.core}>Core 0.1</a> and <a href={specs.core02}>Core 0.2</a></h3>
      <p>
        Goals, capabilities, messages, authority, commitments and custody, and evidence-backed
        outcomes, with the order in which a receiver checks each message and its rejection codes.
        Core 0.2 adds alternatives, <code>met_alternative</code> and <code>declared</code> facts.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Core schemas</h3>
      <p>
        JSON Schema draft 2020-12: the message envelope for A2R and R2R exchanges
        (<a href={specs.coreMessageSchema}>0.1</a>, <a href={specs.coreMessageSchema02}>0.2</a>), and
        the <a href={specs.coreDescriptorSchema}>capability descriptor</a>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>From a task to Core</h3>
      <p>
        How a task's goal becomes a Core <code>propose_goal</code>
        (<a href={specs.exportGoal}><code>export-goal</code></a>), and how
        <a href={specs.delegate}><code>delegate</code></a> runs it on a robot's own plan, with a peer
        robot, commitment, lease and custody (<a href={specs.delegateCore02}>on Core 0.2</a>).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.referenceSimulation}>Reference simulation 0.1</a></h3>
      <p>
        The RFC-001 scenario run by <code>robolang arch02</code>: participants, symbolic world,
        behavior rules and the failure variants.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3>Evidence</h3>
      <p>
        The signed-off exit gates: <a href={specs.foundation1Evidence}>Foundation 1</a>,
        <a href={specs.gate1Evidence}>RL-ARCH-02 gate 1</a> and
        <a href={specs.gate2Evidence}>Gate 2</a>, independent interoperability on Core 0.2.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <h3><a href={specs.authorization}>Physical-run authorization 0.1</a></h3>
      <p>
        A signed cell configuration, task approval and run grant, and the check of the whole chain.
        Even a valid chain ends in <code>A731</code>: physical mode is not enabled.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Orientation</h2>
    <p>
      This summary is informal and only meant to help you find your way around the specification. See
      the <a href="/examples/">Examples</a> page for complete files.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A file contains one or more <strong>tasks</strong>, optionally preceded by a version marker
        such as <code>robolang 2.2;</code>. Without a marker a file is 1.1. From 2.0 a file can also
        contain <strong>missions</strong>, which repeat its tasks within a hard bound.
      </li>
      <li>
        A task has <strong>typed parameters</strong>: <code>robot</code>, <code>object</code>,
        <code>location</code> and <code>region</code>, and from 1.3 also <code>sensor</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A task may carry <strong><code>@safety</code> annotations</strong> and a
        <strong><code>pre</code> block</strong> of conditions, and must have a
        <strong><code>plan</code> block</strong> with at least one statement. From 1.3 it must also
        have a <strong><code>goal</code> block</strong>, which decides whether a run succeeded.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>steps</strong> are <code>move</code>, <code>grasp</code>, <code>place</code>,
        <code>inspect</code>, <code>wait</code> and <code>communicate</code>, plus
        <code>measure</code> from 1.6 and <code>do</code> (a declared action) from 1.8. From 1.4 a
        plan can also branch with <code>if</code> / <code>else</code>, nested at most four deep, and
        end a branch with <code>fail</code>; from 1.5 a step can have its own failure handler.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>conditions</strong> are <code>robot_ready</code>, <code>region_clear</code>,
        <code>object_exists</code> and <code>sensor_ok</code>, and from 1.3 the outcome conditions
        <code>object_at</code>, <code>robot_at</code> and <code>holding</code>. From 1.2 they can be
        combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code> and parentheses. Later
        versions add comparisons of measured values (1.6, with more kinds in 2.2),
        <code>in_state</code> (1.8) and <code>message_received</code> (2.1).
      </li>
      <li>Comments start with <code>//</code> and run to the end of the line.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Diagnostics</h2>
    <p>
      Every problem the tools report has a stable code and a <code>file:line:column</code> position.
      The specification lists each code and its meaning.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Codes</th><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>E1xx</code></td>
            <td>Lexical errors</td>
            <td>An unterminated string</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>E2xx</code></td>
            <td>Syntax errors</td>
            <td><code>E202</code>, a missing semicolon</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>E3xx</code></td>
            <td>Static errors</td>
            <td><code>E303</code>, an undeclared name</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>W4xx</code></td>
            <td>Warnings</td>
            <td><code>W401</code>, <code>max_speed</code> without a unit</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the rules do not cover</h2>
    <p>
      The language specifications define what a well-formed task file is, and the simulation
      specification defines how a task runs against a symbolic world. Neither defines how a named
      location becomes a motion or how a safety limit is enforced on a robot. Those semantics are not
      specified or implemented. Validation covers the text of the program only.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
